('blog-1786720773976', '72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 Exam Date Out', '14 Aug 2026', '3 min read', 'BPSC EXAM CALENDAR', '<h1><font color=\"#f43f5e\">72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 Exam Date Out: Check New Date, Exam Calendar &amp; Preparation Strategy</font></h1><p></p><ul><li><b>72nd BPSC Prelims 2026: </b>Bihar Public Service Commission (BPSC) has released its <a href=\"https://bpsc.bihar.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Examination-Calendar-14.08.2026.pdf\"><b><font color=\"#3b82f6\">revised examination calendar</font></b></a> dated <b>14 August 2026.</b> The biggest update for BPSC aspirants is the new date of the <b>72nd BPSC Preliminary Examination</b>.</li><li>As per the latest calendar, the <b>72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 will be conducted on 25 October 2026.</b>&nbsp;The examination is being conducted for <strong>1,189 vacancies</strong>.</li><li>The Preliminary Examination was earlier scheduled for <strong>26 July 2026</strong>.</li></ul><p></p><h2><br></h2><h2>72nd BPSC Prelims 2026: Important Details</h2><div><div style=\"overflow-x:auto; margin:1rem 0;\"><table style=\"margin: 0px auto; border-width: 1.5px; border-color: rgb(203, 213, 225);\" resize=\"both\"><thead><tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9;\"><th style=\"border-width: 1px; border-color: rgb(203, 213, 225); padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto; width: 393px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Particulars</span></th><th style=\"border-width: 1px; border-color: rgb(203, 213, 225); padding: 10px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Details</span></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Exam</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">72nd BPSC CCE</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Conducting Body</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Bihar Public Service Commission</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Vacancies</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">1,189</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Earlier Prelims Date</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">26 July 2026</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\"><font color=\"#10b981\">New Prelims Date</font></span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\"><font color=\"#10b981\">25 October 2026</font></span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Calendar Released On</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">14 August 2026</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Mains Date</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">To be decided</span></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder; font-size: 14px;\">Interview</span></td><td style=\"border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(226, 232, 240); padding: 10px; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">To be decided</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><table><thead></thead><tbody></tbody></table><span>Candidates should note that the dates mentioned in the examination calendar are tentative wherever specified by BPSC.</span><blockquote><b>72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 New Date</b></blockquote><p></p><ul><li>The <b>72nd BPSC Preliminary Examination is now scheduled for 25 October 2026.</b></li><li>The revised date gives aspirants additional time for syllabus completion, revision and mock-test practice. Candidates should now plan their preparation according to the new examination date instead of waiting for further changes.</li></ul><p></p><blockquote><b>How to Prepare for 72nd BPSC Prelims?</b></blockquote><p>With the exam approaching, candidates should focus on <b>revision and practice rather than collecting new study material.</b></p><h3>1. Focus on BPSC PYQs</h3><p>Previous Year Questions are important for understanding the BPSC question pattern and frequently tested topics. Solve PYQs topic-wise and analyse your mistakes.</p><h3>2. Give Special Attention to Bihar</h3><p>Bihar-specific preparation should remain a major part of the strategy.</p><p>Important areas include:</p><ul><li><p>Bihar History</p></li><li><p>Bihar Geography</p></li><li><p>Bihar Economy</p></li><li><p>Bihar Polity</p></li><li><p>Art &amp; Culture</p></li><li><p>Bihar Current Affairs</p></li><li><p>Government Schemes</p></li></ul><h3>3. Practice Mock Tests</h3><p>Candidates should gradually increase full-length mock-test practice. After every test, analyse incorrect answers, weak topics and unnecessary guesses.</p><h3>4. Revise Current Affairs</h3><p>Along with national and international current affairs, candidates should give special attention to <b>Bihar Current Affairs.</b></p><blockquote><b>Bihar Special Book for 72nd BPSC</b></blockquote><p>For focused Bihar-specific preparation, aspirants can use <b>Bihar Special by Final Attempt. </b>The book is designed to help candidates consolidate important Bihar-related topics for competitive examinations.</p><p>📚<b> Buy Bihar Special by Final Attempt:</b></p><p><a href=\"https://www.amazon.in/dp/8168524624?ref=cm_sw_r_ffobk_cso_wa_apan_dp_RKB18X9HNVVWX7GEAQAV&amp;ref_=cm_sw_r_ffobk_cso_wa_apan_dp_RKB18X9HNVVWX7GEAQAV&amp;social_share=cm_sw_r_ffobk_cso_wa_apan_dp_RKB18X9HNVVWX7GEAQAV&amp;bestFormat=true\">Get Bihar Special Book on Amazon</a>:<font color=\"#3b82f6\">&nbsp;<span style=\"white-space: pre-wrap;\"><a href=\"https://amzn.in/d/08kd4iAo\" style=\"\">https://amzn.in/d/08kd4iAo</a></span></font></p><p class=\"selectable-text copyable-text x15bjb6t x1n2onr6\"><b><br></b></p><blockquote><b>BPSC Revised Exam Calendar 2026</b></blockquote><p></p><ul><li>The latest BPSC calendar dated <b>14 August 2026</b> contains the tentative schedule of several ongoing and upcoming examinations.</li><li>For the<b> 72nd CCE</b>, the key update is:</li><li><b>72nd BPSC Prelims → 25 October 2026</b></li><li>Candidates should regularly check the official BPSC website for further notifications and changes.</li><li><a href=\"https://bpsc.bihar.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Examination-Calendar-14.08.2026.pdf\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Check Official BPSC Exam Calendar</font></a></li></ul><p></p><h2><br></h2><h2><span style=\"font-size: 1.35em;\">FAQs</span></h2><h3>When will the 72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 be held?</h3><p>The 72nd BPSC Preliminary Examination is scheduled for <b>25 October 2026</b> as per the revised examination calendar.</p><h3>How many vacancies are available in 72nd BPSC?</h3><p>The latest calendar mentions <b>1,189 vacancies</b> for the 72nd BPSC CCE.</p><h3>What was the earlier date of 72nd BPSC Prelims?</h3><p>The examination was earlier scheduled for <b>26 July 2026.</b></p><h3>How should I prepare for 72nd BPSC Prelims?</h3><p>Focus on <b>BPSC PYQs, Bihar-specific topics, current affairs, revision and full-length mock tests.</b></p><h3>Which book can help with Bihar-specific preparation?</h3><p>Candidates can refer to <b>Bihar Special by Final Attempt </b>for focused Bihar-specific preparation.</p><p><a href=\"https://amzn.in/d/08kd4iAo\">Buy Bihar Special on Amazon</a></p><p><b>Target Date: 25 October 2026. Plan your preparation accordingly and make every revision count.<br></b></p>', '72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 Exam Date Out: Check New Date & Exam Calendar', '72nd BPSC Prelims 2026, 72nd BPSC Exam Date 2026, 72nd BPSC Prelims Date, BPSC Exam Calendar 2026, BPSC Revised Exam Calendar, 72nd BPSC CCE 2026, BPSC Prelims 2026, 72nd BPSC Vacancy', '72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 is scheduled for 25 October 2026. Check the revised BPSC Exam Calendar, 1,189 vacancies, important dates and preparation strategy.', 'BPSC has released its revised Exam Calendar 2026. As per the latest calendar, the 72nd BPSC Prelims 2026 will be conducted on 25 October 2026 for 1,189 vacancies. Check the new exam date, important schedule and preparation strategy.', NULL, 'https://finalattemptias.com/uploads/images/TTTTTTTTTTT.webp', NULL, 'published', 'Admin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'en', 'both'),
('blog-1786764965646', '80th Independence Day 2026: Theme, History, Vande Mataram & Celebrations', '15 Aug 2026', '5 min read', 'National Events', '<h1 data-path-to-node=\"2\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">80th Independence Day 2026: 15 August, Theme, History, Vande Mataram Commemoration</font></h1><p data-path-to-node=\"3\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"></p><ul><li>India is set to celebrate its <font color=\"#3b82f6\"><b data-path-to-node=\"3\" data-index-in-node=\"30\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">80th Independence Day on August 15, 2026</b>, </font>with immense pride, national fervor and grand celebrations.&nbsp;</li><li>Marking <b data-path-to-node=\"3\" data-index-in-node=\"141\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">79 years of freedom</b> from British colonial rule since August 15, 1947, this historic occasion serves as a bridge connecting India’s glorious freedom struggle with the vision of <b data-path-to-node=\"3\" data-index-in-node=\"317\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Viksit Bharat @2047</b>.</li><li>The 2026 national celebration will highlight indigenous technology, green energy transition, Atmanirbhar defence, active youth participation, and the nationwide <b data-path-to-node=\"4\" data-index-in-node=\"161\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">150-year commemoration of India’s national song, <i data-path-to-node=\"4\" data-index-in-node=\"210\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i></b>.</li></ul><p></p><h2 data-path-to-node=\"6\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\"><br></font></h2><h2 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b><font color=\"#f43f5e\">80th Independence Day 2026: Key Highlights</font></b></h2><ul data-path-to-node=\"7\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Date:</b> Saturday, August 15, 2026</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Edition:</b> 80th Independence Day (Completing 79 Years of Independence)</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Main Venue:</b> Historic Red Fort, New Delhi</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,3,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Led By:</b> Prime Minister Narendra Modi</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"7,4,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"7,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Cultural Significance:</b> In 2026, August 15 coincides with <b data-path-to-node=\"7,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"57\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Navroz (Parsi New Year)</b>, adding a unique layer of cultural diversity and festive spirit to the national celebration.</p></li></ul><h2 data-path-to-node=\"9\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><div style=\"background: rgb(240, 249, 255); border-width: 1.5px 1.5px 1.5px 5px; border-color: rgb(186, 230, 253) rgb(186, 230, 253) rgb(186, 230, 253) rgb(2, 132, 199); padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 14px; margin: 1rem 0px;\">\n          <div style=\"font-size: 13px; color: rgb(3, 105, 161); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(244, 63, 94); font-size: 15px; font-style: italic; font-weight: bolder;\">OFficial Theme for Independence Day 2026:</span></div><div style=\"font-size: 13px; color: rgb(3, 105, 161); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0); font-size: 15px; font-style: italic; font-weight: bolder; letter-spacing: 0.05em;\">The official theme of independence day 2026 centres on two powerful pillars-&nbsp;</span></div><div style=\"font-size: 13px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.05em; font-size: 15px; font-style: italic; font-weight: bolder;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">&nbsp;1. \"</font></span><span style=\"letter-spacing: 0.05em; font-size: 15px; font-style: italic; font-weight: bolder;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">150 years of vande mataram\" </font><font color=\"#000000\">&amp;</font><font color=\"#3b82f6\">&nbsp;</font></span></div><div style=\"font-size: 13px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; margin-bottom: 6px;\"><span style=\"color: rgb(59, 130, 246); font-size: 15px; font-style: italic; font-weight: bolder; letter-spacing: 0.05em;\">&nbsp;2. \"yuva shakti for bharat@2047\"</span></div></div></h2><h2 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b><font color=\"#f43f5e\">Why This Theme Matters:</font></b></h2><ul data-path-to-node=\"13\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"13,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"13,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">150 Years of Vande Mataram:</b> Honors the legacy of the national song composed in 1875, which served as the rallying cry for India\'s independence movement.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"13,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"13,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Yuva Shakti (Youth Power):</b> Focuses on young Indians taking the lead in science, technology, innovation, startups, and nation-building toward a developed India by 2047.</p></li></ul><div><font face=\"Google Sans Text, sans-serif\" color=\"#10b981\"><br></font></div><h2 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b><font color=\"#f43f5e\">What’s Special About Independence Day 2026?</font></b></h2><p data-path-to-node=\"16\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">The 2026 celebrations at the Red Fort and across the nation bring several groundbreaking elements:</p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"17\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">1. First-Ever <i data-path-to-node=\"17\" data-index-in-node=\"14\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i> Rendition at Red Fort Ceremony</font></h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"18\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Historic Milestone:</b> For the first time in Independence Day history, a dedicated rendition of <i data-path-to-node=\"18,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"93\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i> will be performed during the official Red Fort proceedings.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Ceremony Timing:</b> The song will be performed upon the Prime Minister’s arrival, right before the hoisting of the National Flag and the address to the nation.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"18,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"18,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">150th Year Commemoration:</b> The nationwide commemoration runs from <b data-path-to-node=\"18,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"65\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">November 7, 2025, to November 7, 2026</b>.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"19\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">2. Human Formation &amp; Aerial Flower Shower</font></h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"20\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"20,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"20,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">NCC &amp; My Bharat Youth:</b> Around 2,500 NCC cadets and <i data-path-to-node=\"20,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"51\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">My Bharat</i> volunteers will form the words <b data-path-to-node=\"20,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"92\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">‘वंदे मातरम्’</b> on Gyanpath opposite the Red Fort ramparts.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"20,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"20,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Mi-17 Helicopter Tribute:</b> An Indian Air Force Mi-17 helicopter carrying a banner commemorating 150 years of <i data-path-to-node=\"20,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"108\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i> will shower flower petals over the gathering.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"20,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"20,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Nationwide Military Bands:</b> Tri-services and security bands will perform <i data-path-to-node=\"20,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"72\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i>-themed musical tributes across 343 iconic locations from August 8 to August 15, 2026.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"21\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">3. Fully Indigenized Defence Equipment (Atmanirbhar Defence)</font></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"22\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"></p><ul><li>In a major push for self-reliance, <b data-path-to-node=\"22\" data-index-in-node=\"35\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">all military equipment, weaponry, and ceremonial hardware used at the Red Fort ceremony will be 100% indigenously developed in India</b>.</li></ul><p></p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"23\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">4. Real-Time Translation via Digital India Bhashini</font></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"24\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"></p><ul><li>To ensure the Prime Minister’s speech reaches every citizen in their native tongue, the <b data-path-to-node=\"24\" data-index-in-node=\"88\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Bhashini AI platform</b> will provide real-time translation into more than <b data-path-to-node=\"24\" data-index-in-node=\"159\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">22 official Indian languages</b>.</li></ul><p></p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"25\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">5. Focus on Indigenous Tech &amp; Green Transition</font></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"26\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">The national showcase will highlight India’s achievements in:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"27\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"27,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"27,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Advanced Technology:</b> 6G trials, the National Semiconductor Mission, and Artificial Intelligence (AI).</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"27,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"27,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Green Transition:</b> Mission LiFE, solar-powered villages, and the National Green Hydrogen Mission.</p></li></ul><h3 data-path-to-node=\"28\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">6. Har Ghar Tiranga Campaign 2026</font></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"29\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"></p><ul><li>The nationwide <b data-path-to-node=\"29\" data-index-in-node=\"15\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Har Ghar Tiranga</b> initiative continues to encourage citizens across the country to proudly hoist the Indian National Flag at their homes, offices, and institutions, fostering deep personal patriotism.</li></ul><p></p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"30\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">7. International Recognition</font></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"31\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"></p><ul><li>In a landmark gesture strengthening India-US ties, the Governors of <b data-path-to-node=\"31\" data-index-in-node=\"68\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">New York and New Jersey officially proclaimed August 15, 2026, as \"India Independence Day\"</b>, recognizing the immense contributions of the Indian-American community.</li></ul><p></p><p data-path-to-node=\"31\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><br></p><h2 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b style=\"\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">History of Indian Independence (1947 to 2026)</font></b></h2><p data-path-to-node=\"34\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">India achieved independence on August 15, 1947, following a prolonged and courageous fight against nearly 200 years of British colonial rule.</p><h3 data-path-to-node=\"35\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Key Milestones in the Freedom Struggle:</font></h3><ul data-path-to-node=\"36\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Revolt of 1857:</b> The First War of Independence served as the early armed challenge against British East India Company rule.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Formation of INC (1885):</b> The Indian National Congress provided a structured national political platform.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Prominent Freedom Fighters:</b> Visionaries like Mahatma Gandhi, Subhas Chandra Bose, Bhagat Singh, Bal Gangadhar Tilak, Jawaharlal Nehru, Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, and Sarojini Naidu rallied millions.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,3,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Mass Movements:</b> Non-Cooperation, Civil Disobedience, and Satyagraha transformed the struggle into a nationwide mass movement.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,4,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Quit India Movement (1942):</b> Gandhi’s decisive <b data-path-to-node=\"36,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"46\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">\"Do or Die\"</b> call demanded an immediate end to British rule.</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"36,5,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"36,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Indian Independence Act 1947:</b> Formulated under the Mountbatten Plan, the act passed British Parliament in July 1947, granting India full sovereignty on the midnight of <b data-path-to-node=\"36,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"168\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">August 14–15, 1947</b>.</p></li></ul><div><font face=\"Google Sans Text, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><h2 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><b><font color=\"#f43f5e\">79th vs 80th Independence Day: Clearing the Confusion</font></b></h2><p data-path-to-node=\"39\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">A common point of confusion is whether 2026 marks the 79th or 80th Independence Day:</p><ul data-path-to-node=\"40\" style=\"padding-inline-start: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"40,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"40,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">80th Independence Day (2026):</b> India celebrates its <b data-path-to-node=\"40,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"51\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">80th Independence Day</b> on August 15, 2026 (counting August 15, 1947, as the 1st).</p></li><li style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><p data-path-to-node=\"40,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"40,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">79 Years Completed:</b> On this day, India officially completes <b data-path-to-node=\"40,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"60\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">79 full years</b> of sovereign independence.</p></li></ul><div><font face=\"Google Sans Text, sans-serif\"><br></font></div><h2 data-path-to-node=\"42\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">Independence Day vs. Republic Day: The Key Differences</font></h2><table data-path-to-node=\"43\" style=\"margin-bottom: 32px; font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><thead style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><strong style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Feature</font></strong></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><strong style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Independence Day (15 August)</font></strong></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><strong style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Republic Day (26 January)</font></strong></td></tr></thead><tbody style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,1,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,1,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Historical Event</b></span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,1,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Marks freedom from British Rule in 1947.</span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,1,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Marks the adoption of the Constitution of India in 1950.</span></td></tr><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,2,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,2,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Main Venue</b></span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,2,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,2,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Red Fort</b>, New Delhi.</span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,2,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,2,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Kartavya Path</b> (formerly Rajpath), New Delhi.</span></td></tr><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,3,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,3,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Flag Ceremony</b></span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,3,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">The <b data-path-to-node=\"43,3,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"4\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Prime Minister</b> hoists the National Flag.</span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,3,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">The <b data-path-to-node=\"43,3,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"4\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">President</b> unfurls the National Flag.</span></td></tr><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,4,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,4,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Chief Guest</b></span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,4,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Generally no foreign chief guest is invited.</span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,4,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">A foreign Head of State/Government is invited as Chief Guest.</span></td></tr><tr style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,5,0,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"43,5,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Focus</b></span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,5,1,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">National development goals and patriotic tribute.</span></td><td style=\"border-width: 1px; border-style: solid; border-color: rgb(196, 199, 197); padding: 8px 12px; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><span data-path-to-node=\"43,5,2,0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Grand military parade, cultural tableaux, and constitutional democracy.</span></td></tr></tbody></table><h3 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important; margin-top: 0px !important;\"><span style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif;\">The </span><b data-path-to-node=\"46\" data-index-in-node=\"4\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif; font-size: inherit; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">80th Independence Day in 2026</b><span style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif;\"> is not just a tribute to the heroic sacrifices of the past, but a springboard for the future. Blending the timeless <font color=\"#3b82f6\">legacy of </font></span><font color=\"#3b82f6\"><i data-path-to-node=\"46\" data-index-in-node=\"160\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif; font-size: inherit; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Vande Mataram</i><span style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif;\"> with the energy of </span><i data-path-to-node=\"46\" data-index-in-node=\"193\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif; font-size: inherit; line-height: 1.15 !important;\">Yuva Shakti</i></font><span style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif;\">, India continues its confident journey toward becoming a developed, self-reliant nation by 2047.</span></h3><p data-path-to-node=\"46\" style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><br></p><h1 style=\"font-family: &quot;Google Sans Text&quot;, sans-serif !important; line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><b data-path-to-node=\"47\" data-index-in-node=\"0\" style=\"line-height: 1.15 !important;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">Happy 80th Independence Day! Jai Hind!</font></b></h1>', '', '80th Independence Day 2026, 15 August 2026, Independence Day Theme 2026, Vande Mataram 150 years, Red Fort Celebrations 2026, Yuva Shakti Viksit Bharat 2047, Har Ghar Tiranga 2026, Indian Independence History, Difference between Independence Day and Republic Day, Independence Day 2026 FAQs', 'Complete guide to India\'s 80th Independence Day on 15 August 2026. Discover the official theme, 150 years of Vande Mataram, Red Fort highlights, and FAQs.', 'India will mark its historic 80th Independence Day on August 15, 2026, completing 79 years of freedom. Honoring the 150th anniversary of Vande Mataram alongside the vision of Viksit Bharat @2047, this comprehensive guide covers the official theme, historical milestones, indigenized Red Fort celebrations, and key FAQs.', NULL, 'https://finalattemptias.com/uploads/images/15_august.webp', NULL, 'published', 'Admin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'en', 'both'),
('blog-1786772537557', 'BPSC Exam Calendar 2026', '15 Aug 2026', '2 min read', 'Exam Calendar', '⬇️<b><font color=\"#3b82f6\"><a href=\"https://bpsc.bihar.gov.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Examination-Calendar-14.08.2026.pdf\">Download Official BPSC Exam Calendar 2026 (PDF)</a></font></b>', 'BPSC Exam Calendar 2026 PDF: Check 72nd CCE & Exam Dates', 'BPSC Exam Calendar 2026, BPSC 72nd CCE Prelims Date, BPSC Calendar PDF Download, BPSC Exam Schedule 2026, Bihar Public Service Commission Exam Dates', 'BPSC Exam Calendar 2026 is released! Check tentative prelims & mains exam dates for 72nd CCE, TRE 4.0, Judicial Services, and download the official PDF schedule.', '', NULL, 'https://finalattemptias.com/uploads/images/EXAM_CAL.webp', NULL, 'published', 'Admin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'en', 'both');
INSERT INTO `blogs` (`id`, `title`, `publishDate`, `readTime`, `category`, `content`, `seoTitle`, `seoKeywords`, `seoDescription`, `blurb`, `slug`, `imageUrl`, `excerpt`, `status`, `author_name`, `author_image`, `title_hi`, `content_hi`, `blurb_hi`, `language`, `publish_target`) VALUES
('blog-1786886615791', '16th Finance Commission ', '16 Aug 2026', '5 min read', 'Polity', '<h1><font color=\"#f43f5e\">16th Finance Commission and the Future of Indian Federalism</font></h1><h2><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Introduction</font></h2><p></p><ul><li>India’s federal system is built on a delicate balance between <strong>national economic priorities and State-level autonomy</strong>. At the heart of this relationship lies <strong>fiscal federalism-</strong>the constitutional and institutional framework through which taxation powers, expenditure responsibilities, borrowing powers and financial transfers are distributed among different levels of government.</li><li>The <strong>16th Finance Commission (FC-16)</strong>, chaired by <strong>Dr. Arvind Panagariya</strong>, has introduced important changes for the award period <strong>2026–31</strong>. While retaining the States’ share in the divisible pool at <strong>41%</strong>, it has significantly altered the approach to horizontal devolution by introducing a <strong>10% weight for contribution to GDP</strong>, discontinuing Post-Devolution Revenue Deficit Grants, and substantially increasing transfers to local governments.</li></ul><p></p><p>These recommendations raise a fundamental question for Indian federalism:</p><blockquote><p><strong style=\"font-style: normal;\"><font color=\"#000000\">Should fiscal transfers primarily equalise the fiscal capacity of States, or should they increasingly reward economic performance, fiscal discipline and contribution to national growth?</font></strong></p></blockquote><p><font color=\"#10b981\">The answer will shape the future of <strong>cooperative federalism, competitive federalism and fiscal autonomy in India</strong>.</font></p><h2><font color=\"#3b82f6\">What is Fiscal Federalism?</font></h2><p></p><ul><li><strong>Fiscal federalism</strong> refers to the distribution of <strong>taxation powers, expenditure responsibilities, borrowing powers and intergovernmental transfers</strong> among different levels of government.</li></ul><p></p><p><b>India’s fiscal federalism attempts to address three major forms of imbalance:</b></p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Type of Imbalance</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Meaning</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Indian Context</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Vertical Fiscal Imbalance</strong></td><td>Difference between revenue-raising capacity and expenditure responsibilities</td><td>The Union controls many broad-based and buoyant taxes, while States handle health, education, agriculture, policing and infrastructure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Horizontal Fiscal Imbalance</strong></td><td>Differences in fiscal capacity and expenditure needs among States</td><td>States differ significantly in income, geography, demographics, resources and administrative capacity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Third-Tier Fiscal Imbalance</strong></td><td>Weak financial capacity of local governments</td><td>Panchayats and Municipalities have major service-delivery responsibilities but limited own-source revenues</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><font color=\"#000000\">For instance, a State such as <strong>Bihar</strong> cannot necessarily provide the same level of public services as a high-income State simply by imposing similar tax rates because its revenue-raising capacity and expenditure requirements are different.</font></blockquote><h2><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Constitutional Framework of Fiscal Federalism in India</font></h2><p>The Constitution provides the institutional foundation for Centre-State fiscal relations.</p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Constitutional Provision</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Significance</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Article 246 &amp; 7th Schedule</strong></td><td>Divides legislative and taxation powers between Union and States</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 246A</strong></td><td>Provides concurrent power to Parliament and State Legislatures concerning GST</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 270</strong></td><td>Provides the basis for distribution of shareable Union taxes between Centre and States</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 275</strong></td><td>Provides for grants-in-aid to States in need of assistance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 280</strong></td><td>Provides for the Finance Commission</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 282</strong></td><td>Enables Union and States to provide grants for public purposes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Articles 243-I &amp; 243-Y</strong></td><td>Provide for State Finance Commissions for Panchayats and Municipalities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 293</strong></td><td>Deals with State borrowing and Union consent in specified circumstances</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Article 279A</strong></td><td>Establishes the GST Council</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The <strong>Finance Commission</strong>, constituted by the President every five years or earlier under <strong>Article 280</strong>, recommends both vertical devolution between the Union and States and horizontal distribution among individual States. It also recommends principles governing grants-in-aid and measures to augment State Consolidated Funds for Panchayats and Municipalities.</p><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Key Recommendations of the 16th Finance Commission</font></h1><h2>1. States’ Share Retained at 41%</h2><p></p><ul><li>The 16th Finance Commission has retained the States’ share in the divisible pool at <strong>41%</strong>, the same level recommended by the 15th Finance Commission.</li><li>This provides <strong>continuity and predictability</strong> in Centre-State fiscal transfers.</li><li>However, the decision has also generated debate because several States had demanded a higher share, with <strong>18 States seeking an increase to 50%</strong> while some others sought a share in the range of 45–48%.</li></ul><p></p><h2>2. Major Change in Horizontal Devolution Formula</h2><p></p><ul><li>One of the most significant changes is the revision of the criteria used to distribute the States’ share among individual States.</li></ul><p></p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Criterion</font></th><th align=\"right\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">15th Finance Commission</font></th><th align=\"right\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">16th Finance Commission</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Change</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Income Distance</strong></td><td align=\"right\">45%</td><td align=\"right\">42.5%</td><td>Reduced</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Population (2011)</strong></td><td align=\"right\">15%</td><td align=\"right\">17.5%</td><td>Increased</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Demographic Performance</strong></td><td align=\"right\">12.5%</td><td align=\"right\">10%</td><td>Reduced</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Area</strong></td><td align=\"right\">15%</td><td align=\"right\">10%</td><td>Reduced</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Forest</strong></td><td align=\"right\">10%</td><td align=\"right\">10%</td><td>Retained</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tax &amp; Fiscal Effort</strong></td><td align=\"right\">2.5%</td><td align=\"right\">Nil</td><td>Removed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contribution to GDP</strong></td><td align=\"right\">Nil</td><td align=\"right\">10%</td><td>New</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><font color=\"#000000\">The new formula represents an important philosophical shift-from a primarily <strong>redistributive approach towards a combination of equity and economic performance</strong>.</font></blockquote><h1>3. 10% Weight for Contribution to GDP</h1><p></p><ul><li>The introduction of a <strong>10% weight for States’ contribution to GDP</strong> is perhaps the most debated feature of FC-16.</li><li>The criterion uses the <strong>square root of State GSDP</strong> to moderate the advantage that could otherwise accrue disproportionately to the largest State economies.&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;</li></ul><p></p><div style=\"background: #f0f9ff; border: 1.5px solid #bae6fd; border-left: 5px solid #0284c7; padding: 1.25rem; border-radius: 14px; margin: 1rem 0;\"><h1 style=\"background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><font style=\"\" color=\"#f43f5e\">Why is this important?</font></h1><p style=\"font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li>The measure recognises the argument made by relatively prosperous and industrialised States that a purely redistribution-oriented formula may not sufficiently recognise their contribution to national economic growth.</li><li>Thus, FC-16 attempts to balance:</li></ul><p></p><p style=\"font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><span style=\"font-weight: bolder;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</font><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Equity + Efficiency + Economic Contribution</font></span></p><blockquote style=\"font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><b style=\"font-style: normal;\"><font color=\"#f43f5e\">However, this also creates a potential tension. Economic output is partly influenced by historical advantages such as:</font></b></blockquote><ul style=\"background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><li><p>Infrastructure</p></li><li><p>Urbanisation</p></li><li><p>Market access</p></li><li><p>Industrialisation</p></li><li><p>Coastline and geographical advantages</p></li><li><p>Existing economic agglomeration</p></li></ul><p style=\"font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">Therefore, rewarding GDP contribution without maintaining a strong equalisation mechanism could potentially reinforce existing regional inequalities.</p></div><h1>4. Discontinuation of Revenue Deficit Grants</h1><p>The 16th Finance Commission has discontinued <strong>Post-Devolution Revenue Deficit Grants (RDGs)</strong>.&nbsp;</p><p><span>It has also discontinued:</span></p><ul><li><p>Sector-Specific Grants</p></li><li><p>State-Specific Grants</p></li></ul><p>The underlying concern is that persistent deficit-filling transfers could create <strong>moral hazard</strong>, reducing incentives for States to mobilise revenues, rationalise subsidies and control revenue expenditure</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto; margin:1rem 0;\"><table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:0 auto; border:1.5px solid #cbd5e1;\" resize=\"both\"><thead><tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9;\"><th style=\"border: 1px solid rgb(203, 213, 225); padding: 10px; text-align: left; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; resize: horizontal; overflow: auto; width: 243px;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">The Positive Argument</font></th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1; padding:10px; text-align:left; font-weight:bold; font-size:12px; resize:horizontal; overflow:auto;\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">The Concern</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #e2e8f0; padding:10px; font-size:12px; resize:horizontal; overflow:auto;\"><font color=\"#000000\">Removing such grants may encourage:</font><ul style=\"font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><li style=\"\"><p><font color=\"#000000\">Fiscal discipline</font></p></li><li style=\"\"><p><font color=\"#000000\">Better revenue mobilisation</font></p></li><li style=\"\"><p><font color=\"#000000\">Expenditure rationalisation</font></p></li><li style=\"\"><p style=\"\"><font color=\"#000000\">Greater accountability</font></p></li></ul></td><td style=\"border:1px solid #e2e8f0; padding:10px; font-size:12px; resize:horizontal; overflow:auto;\">Not every fiscal deficit is necessarily the result of poor financial management. States may face structural disadvantages because of:<ul style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); font-size: 15px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"><li><p>Difficult geography</p></li><li><p>Low taxable capacity</p></li><li><p>Border responsibilities</p></li><li><p>Demographic pressures</p></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.015em;\">The discontinuation of RDGs therefore raises concerns about whether structurally weaker States will have adequate fiscal protection.</span></div><h1>5. Greater Focus on Local Governments</h1><p></p><ul><li>FC-16 has significantly increased the recommended allocation for <strong>rural and urban local governments</strong>.</li><li>It recommends <strong>₹7.91 lakh crore for 2026–31</strong>, compared with approximately <strong>₹4.36 lakh crore</strong> recommended by FC-15.</li><li>Basic and performance grants are generally structured in an <strong>80:20 ratio</strong>.</li><li>The recommendations also include:</li></ul><blockquote><ul><li><font color=\"#000000\"><strong style=\"font-size: inherit;\">₹56,100 crore</strong><span> as Special Infrastructure Grants for wastewater management in selected cities.</span></font></li><li><font color=\"#000000\"><strong style=\"font-size: inherit;\">₹10,000 crore</strong><span> Urbanisation Premium for transitioning peri-urban areas into urban local bodies.</span></font></li></ul></blockquote><p></p><p>This strengthens the financial foundation of the <strong>third tier of government</strong>, although the effectiveness of such transfers will also depend on stronger State Finance Commissions and local institutional capacity.</p><h1>6. Strengthening Disaster-Management Financing</h1><p>FC-16 recommends an overall corpus of <strong>₹2,04,401 crore</strong> for State Disaster Response and Mitigation Funds.</p><p>The estimated Union contribution is <strong>₹1,55,916 crore</strong>.</p><p>The recommended Centre-State sharing ratio is:</p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Category of States</font></th><th align=\"right\"><font color=\"#3b82f6\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; Centre : State</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Northeastern &amp; Himalayan States</td><td align=\"right\"><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; 90 : 10</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Other States</td><td align=\"right\"><strong>75 : 25</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>This recognises the higher disaster vulnerability and geographical challenges faced by several States.</p><h1>7. Stronger Fiscal Responsibility Framework</h1><p>FC-16 has placed greater emphasis on fiscal sustainability.</p><p>Key recommendations include:</p><ul><li><p>States should maintain fiscal deficits within <strong>3% of GSDP</strong>.</p></li><li><p>The Union should progressively reduce its fiscal deficit to <strong>3.5% of GDP by 2030–31</strong>.</p></li><li><p>Off-budget borrowing should be discontinued and transparently brought onto government budgets.</p></li><li><p>Poorly targeted subsidies should be rationalised.</p></li><li><p>Inactive and persistently loss-making State public enterprises should be reviewed.</p></li></ul><p>The Commission projects combined Centre-State debt to decline from <strong>77.3% of GDP in 2026–27 to 73.1% in 2030–31</strong>.</p><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Major Concerns for the Future of Indian Fiscal Federalism</font></h1><h2>1. Persistent Vertical Fiscal Imbalance</h2><p></p><ul><li>The Union continues to control many buoyant tax instruments, while States remain responsible for several expenditure-intensive public services.</li><li>This creates a structural mismatch between <strong>revenue capacity and expenditure responsibility</strong>.</li><li>The debate over increasing States’ share to 50% therefore remains central to India’s fiscal federalism.</li></ul><p></p><h2>2. Cesses and Surcharges and the Shrinking Divisible Pool</h2><p>A major concern is the increasing role of <strong>cesses and surcharges</strong>, which are excluded from the divisible pool.</p><p>The share of the divisible pool in Gross Tax Revenue declined from <strong>89.1% in 2014–15 to roughly 74–80% during 2020–24</strong>.</p><p>The issue is not simply whether cesses and surcharges are constitutional. The deeper concern relates to their:</p><ul><li><p>Scale</p></li><li><p>Duration</p></li><li><p>Transparency</p></li><li><p>Impact on the shareable tax base</p></li></ul><p>A shrinking divisible pool can reduce the effective resources available to States even when the nominal devolution percentage remains unchanged.</p><h2>3. Equity vs Efficiency</h2><p></p><ul><li>The introduction of GDP contribution creates a new debate:</li><li><strong>Should States be rewarded for producing more, or should transfers primarily help States that have less fiscal capacity?</strong></li><li>A purely equity-oriented system may frustrate economically productive States.</li><li>On the other hand, an excessive focus on economic output could create a <strong>cumulative advantage for already prosperous States</strong>.</li><li>Therefore, India\'s fiscal architecture must find a balance between:</li></ul><p></p><p><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<font color=\"#f43f5e\"> Equalisation → Efficiency → Accountability → Growth</font></strong></p><h2>4. Reduced Space for State-Specific Needs</h2><p></p><ul><li>India\'s States face very different development challenges.</li><li>For example:</li></ul><p></p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">State/Region</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Major Challenge</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assam</td><td>Floods and disaster vulnerability</td></tr><tr><td>Uttarakhand</td><td>Himalayan geography</td></tr><tr><td>Rajasthan</td><td>Drought and water scarcity</td></tr><tr><td>Maharashtra</td><td>Urbanisation and industrial pressures</td></tr><tr><td>Bihar</td><td>High population pressure and comparatively lower fiscal capacity</td></tr></tbody></table><p></p><ul><li>A uniform formula cannot fully capture such diverse expenditure needs.</li><li>The discontinuation of State-specific and sector-specific grants therefore raises concerns about the ability of the transfer system to respond to <strong>asymmetrical State-level requirements</strong>.</li></ul><p></p><h1>5. Weak State Finance Commissions</h1><p>India\'s third-tier governments cannot achieve genuine fiscal autonomy merely through larger transfers from the Union Finance Commission.</p><p>State Finance Commissions often face:</p><ul><li><p>Delayed constitution</p></li><li><p>Delayed reports</p></li><li><p>Weak implementation</p></li><li><p>Limited administrative capacity</p></li></ul><p>Consequently, increasing Union-level grants without strengthening the State Finance Commission mechanism could produce <strong>top-down decentralisation rather than genuine local fiscal autonomy</strong>.</p><h1>6. GST and State Fiscal Autonomy</h1><p></p><ul><li>The introduction of GST created a common national indirect-tax market and reduced tax cascading.</li><li>However, it also pooled important taxation powers of the Union and States within the <strong>GST Council under Article 279A</strong>.</li><li>States therefore have less unilateral flexibility over major consumption taxes than they had before GST.</li><li>At the same time, the Supreme Court\'s decision in <strong>Union of India v. Mohit Minerals (2022)</strong> emphasised that GST Council recommendations have persuasive rather than binding value, highlighting the cooperative as well as contested nature of Indian federalism.</li></ul><p></p><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">What Can India Learn from Global Fiscal Federalism?</font></h1><p></p><ul><li>International experience shows that <strong>equalisation and incentives do not have to be mutually exclusive</strong>.</li></ul><p></p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Country</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Key Approach</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Lesson for India</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Australia</strong></td><td>Horizontal Fiscal Equalisation</td><td>Transfers can compensate States for differences in revenue capacity and expenditure needs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Canada</strong></td><td>Unconditional Equalisation Transfers</td><td>Weaker provinces can receive transfers without excessive expenditure micromanagement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>India</strong></td><td>Finance Commission-based devolution</td><td>Can combine equalisation with performance and fiscal discipline</td></tr></tbody></table><blockquote><ul><li><font color=\"#000000\">Australia\'s Commonwealth Grants Commission explicitly seeks to enable States to provide broadly comparable public services by accounting for differences in revenue capacity and expenditure needs. Canada\'s Equalization Program similarly provides unconditional transfers to provinces with weaker revenue-raising capacity.</font></li><li><font color=\"#000000\">For India, the key lesson is that <strong>fiscal equalisation can coexist with State autonomy and performance incentives</strong>.</font></li></ul></blockquote><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">How Can India Strengthen Fiscal Federalism?</font></h1><h2>1. Preserve an Explicit Equalisation Mechanism</h2><p></p><ul><li>India should retain a clearly identifiable equalisation component in intergovernmental transfers.</li><li>If traditional Revenue Deficit Grants are considered distortionary, they could be replaced with a <strong>transparent equalisation grant</strong> based on measurable fiscal capacity and expenditure needs.</li></ul><p></p><h2>2. Rationalise Cesses and Surcharges</h2><p>Long-running cesses and surcharges should be periodically reviewed.</p><p>A stronger fiscal bargain between the Union and States could involve:</p><ul><li><p>Greater transparency</p></li><li><p>Periodic review</p></li><li><p>Disclosure of collections</p></li><li><p>Disclosure of utilisation</p></li><li><p>Disclosure of unspent balances</p></li><li><p>Clear timelines for major cesses</p></li></ul><p>Where their original purpose has effectively become permanent, they could progressively be incorporated into ordinary shareable taxes.</p><h2>3. Reward Performance Without Sacrificing Equity</h2><p>Economic contribution can legitimately form part of horizontal devolution, but it should remain <strong>supplementary to equalisation</strong>.</p><p>Future formulas could also reward:</p><ul><li><p>Fiscal effort</p></li><li><p>Improvement in tax capacity</p></li><li><p>Human-development outcomes</p></li><li><p>Environmental services</p></li><li><p>Governance and institutional performance</p></li></ul><p>This would create incentives without disproportionately favouring States that already possess larger economic bases.</p><h2>4. Strengthen State Finance Commissions</h2><p>State Finance Commissions should be constituted within constitutional timelines.</p><p>Their recommendations should be:</p><ul><li><p>Tabled before State Legislatures</p></li><li><p>Publicly disclosed</p></li><li><p>Followed by action-taken reports</p></li><li><p>Supported with adequate technical capacity</p></li></ul><p>This is essential for transforming decentralisation into <strong>genuine local fiscal autonomy</strong>.</p><h2>5. Reduce Excessive Dependence on Centrally Sponsored Schemes</h2><p></p><ul><li>Centrally Sponsored Schemes serve important national objectives, but excessive earmarking can reduce States\' flexibility.</li><li>The Union should periodically rationalise overlapping schemes and provide States with greater flexibility in programme design while retaining national priorities where necessary.</li></ul><p></p><h2>6. Improve Fiscal Transparency</h2><p>Both the Union and States should comprehensively disclose:</p><ul><li><p>Off-budget liabilities</p></li><li><p>Government guarantees</p></li><li><p>Public-sector enterprise liabilities</p></li><li><p>Major subsidy commitments</p></li></ul><p>A common fiscal-accounting framework can improve transparency and prevent governments from shifting liabilities outside headline deficit figures.</p><h2>7. Account for Climate and Geographical Vulnerability</h2><p>Future Finance Commissions should develop better measures of:</p><ul><li><p>Disaster exposure</p></li><li><p>Mountainous terrain</p></li><li><p>Coastal vulnerability</p></li><li><p>Ecological services</p></li><li><p>Climate-adaptation costs</p></li></ul><p>Environmental protection and climate resilience often generate benefits for the entire country. Therefore, the fiscal burden should not fall disproportionately on States that provide these national public goods.</p><h2>8. Institutionalise Continuous Centre-State Dialogue</h2><p>Fiscal federalism should not be treated as a negotiation that happens only once every five years.</p><p>Regular dialogue should be strengthened through institutions such as:</p><ul><li><p>GST Council</p></li><li><p>Inter-State Council</p></li><li><p>NITI Aayog Governing Council</p></li><li><p>State Finance Ministers\' forums</p></li></ul><p>A continuous fiscal dialogue can help India manage emerging challenges in taxation, debt, climate change, urbanisation and public expenditure.</p><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">The Future of Indian Federalism: From Devolution to a Fiscal Compact</font></h1><p></p><ul><li>The 16th Finance Commission marks an important evolution in India\'s fiscal federal architecture.</li><li>Its approach attempts to move beyond a simple <strong>\"Centre gives, States receive\"</strong> framework towards a system that places greater emphasis on:</li></ul><p></p><p><strong>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; <font color=\"#f43f5e\">Economic Contribution + Fiscal Discipline + Performance + Local Governments</font></strong></p><p><ul><li>However, the success of this approach will depend on whether India can preserve the principle of <strong>fiscal equalisation</strong>.</li><li>The fundamental challenge is to ensure that a State\'s ability to provide essential public services does not depend overwhelmingly on its existing economic strength.</li></ul><span><b>A sustainable model of Indian federalism therefore needs to combine:</b></span></p><table><thead><tr><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Principle</font></th><th><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Objective</font></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Equity</strong></td><td>Reduce disparities in fiscal capacity</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Efficiency</strong></td><td>Reward productive economic performance</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fiscal Discipline</strong></td><td>Prevent unsustainable debt and deficits</td></tr><tr><td><strong>State Autonomy</strong></td><td>Preserve flexibility in expenditure priorities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Local Empowerment</strong></td><td>Strengthen Panchayats and Municipalities</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Transparency</strong></td><td>Improve accountability of public finances</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cooperation</strong></td><td>Build consensus between Union and States</td></tr></tbody></table><h1><font color=\"#3b82f6\">Conclusion</font></h1><p></p><ul><li>The <strong>16th Finance Commission</strong> represents a significant shift in the philosophy of India\'s fiscal federalism. By retaining <strong>41% vertical devolution</strong>, introducing a <strong>10% GDP contribution criterion</strong>, discontinuing Revenue Deficit Grants and expanding local government transfers, it attempts to combine <strong>equity, efficiency and fiscal discipline</strong>.</li><li>Yet, the future of Indian federalism cannot be determined by the size of transfers alone. The deeper question is whether India\'s fiscal system can simultaneously encourage <strong>economic growth in successful States</strong> and protect the fiscal capacity of States facing structural disadvantages.</li><li>The way forward is therefore not a choice between <strong>competitive federalism and cooperative federalism</strong>, or between <strong>equity and efficiency</strong>.</li><li>India needs a durable <strong>fiscal compact</strong> that rewards performance while preserving equalisation, strengthens State autonomy while ensuring fiscal responsibility, and empowers local governments while maintaining national priorities.</li><li>In the long run, the strength of Indian federalism will depend not merely on <strong>how much money is devolved</strong>, but on <strong>how fairly, transparently and effectively public resources are distributed across the Union, States and local governments</strong>.</li></ul><div><p></p></div><p></p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto; margin:1rem 0;\"><table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:0 auto; border:1.5px solid #cbd5e1;\" resize=\"both\"><thead><tr style=\"background:#f1f5f9;\"><th style=\"border:1px solid #cbd5e1; padding:10px; text-align:left; font-weight:bold; font-size:12px; resize:horizontal; overflow:auto;\"><h1><font color=\"#f43f5e\">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</font></h1><h3 style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">1. What is the States\' share recommended by the 16th Finance Commission?</h3><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li>The 16th Finance Commission has retained the States\' share at <span style=\"font-weight: bolder;\">41% of the divisible pool</span>, the same as the 15th Finance Commission.</li></ul><p></p><h3 style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">2. What is the major change in the horizontal devolution formula?</h3><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li>The 16th Finance Commission has introduced a <span style=\"font-weight: bolder;\">10% weight for States\' contribution to GDP</span>, calculated using the square root of State GSDP.</li></ul><p></p><h3 style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">3. Why has the discontinuation of Revenue Deficit Grants been controversial?</h3><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li>It removes an important fiscal cushion for structurally disadvantaged States and shifts the emphasis from deficit-filling transfers towards <span style=\"font-weight: bolder;\">fiscal discipline and incentives</span>.</li></ul><p></p><h3 style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">4. Which Constitutional Article deals with the Finance Commission?</h3><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li><span style=\"font-weight: bolder;\">Article 280</span> provides for the constitution of the Finance Commission by the President every five years or earlier and defines its major responsibilities.</li></ul><p></p><h3 style=\"color: rgb(15, 23, 42); text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\">5. How do cesses and surcharges affect fiscal federalism?</h3><p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 400; letter-spacing: normal; text-transform: none; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);\"></p><ul><li>Cesses and surcharges are excluded from the divisible pool, so their increasing use can reduce the effective pool of Union tax revenue available for sharing with States, thereby intensifying concerns regarding vertical fiscal imbalance.</li></ul><p></p></th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>', '16th Finance Commission and the Future of Indian Federalism', '16th Finance Commission, 16th Finance Commission recommendations, Finance Commission 2026, fiscal federalism in India, Indian federalism, Centre-State financial relations, tax devolution, horizontal devolution, vertical devolution, 41 percent devolution, GDP contribution criterion, Revenue Deficit Grants, cesses and surcharges, State fiscal autonomy, cooperative federalism, competitive federalism, local government finance', 'Explore the 16th Finance Commission\'s recommendations, 41% tax devolution, GDP-based criteria, fiscal federalism, State autonomy and the future of Centre-State relations in India.', '', NULL, 'https://finalattemptias.com/uploads/images/16th_Finance_Commission.webp', NULL, 'published', 'Admin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'en', 'both');
INSERT INTO `CompanyValue` (`id`, `type`, `title`, `content`, `createdAt`, `updatedAt`) VALUES
('473c2a7c-fa4b-4390-b4de-0b7cc71a8567', 'VISION', 'Our Vision', 'To create a support system where every student receives standard guidance to crack civil service exams in their final attempt, raising the selection rates in Bihar.', '2026-07-18 13:13:03.763', '2026-07-18 13:13:03.763'),
